        "Additional NotebookLM notes to add",
        "• The Landscape of the Dead: We are standing on a necropolis - a literal \"city of the dead\" - located at the outskirts of ancient Roman Melite. The site spans approximately 2,166 square metres, making it the largest complex in Malta.",
        "• Historical Timeline: Roman rule in Malta began in 218 BCE during the Second Punic War, but these catacombs were mainly constructed between the 4th and 8th centuries CE. They were later abandoned and reused in the 13th century during the re-Christianisation of the island.",
        "• Archaeological Origins: The site evolved from earlier Phoenician and Romano-Punic shaft-and-chamber tombs. These tombs were packed so closely together that chambers often broke into one another, encouraging the later connection of spaces into long, narrow corridors.",
        "• The Discovery: Antonio Bosio, born in Malta in 1575, can be presented as the “Christopher Columbus” of the catacombs because of his lifelong study of Roman catacombs. The systematic clearance and investigation of St Paul’s Catacombs was led by Dr A. A. Caruana in 1894.",
        "• The Population: Estimates suggest between 1,000 and 5,000 people were buried here. Historians rely heavily on archaeology because Malta was a small and rarely documented corner of the Roman Empire.",
        "Additional YouTube notes to add",
        "The Rabat catacombs fascinated visitors, antiquarians and archaeologists for centuries because many remained accessible until relatively recent times.",
        "Early written references begin with Marc Antonio Axiaq around 1610, who described rock-cut burial spaces with pits for corpses and smaller holes for infants. Francesco Abela later gave a fuller account in Descrittione di Malta (1647), distinguishing pagan and Christian hypogea and recording that St Paul’s Catacombs were accessible through a small hole. He even took visiting dignitaries through the tunnels.",
        "Later writers included Onorato Bres, G. P. Badger, Achille Ferris, Cesare Vassallo and Antonio Annetto Caruana. Caruana’s 1894 cleaning, study and survey of St Paul’s Catacombs was especially important, even though he still believed the catacombs were mainly Phoenician. His work recorded the condition of the site before later interventions.",
        "International interest increased after Caruana. Albert Mayr visited Malta at the end of the nineteenth and beginning of the twentieth century, and later German scholarship studied the Christian and Jewish catacombs. Sir Themistocles Zammit also excavated many tombs in the wider Taċ-Ċagħqi cemetery zone.",
        "Professor Mario Buhagiar’s 1986 study became a key modern reference for Maltese catacombs, but because the site had long been open to the public, little physical excavation work had been carried out for many years.",
        "Heritage Malta later launched a €4.2 million conservation and interpretation project, 85% funded by the European Regional Development Fund. The project included archaeological investigations, environmental studies, conservation work and major visitor improvements. The number of catacombs open to the public increased from two to more than twenty, across around 10,000 square metres, with eight main interpretation points.",
        "The wider Rabat and Mdina area was attractive for settlement because of its central, defensible position, water supply and fertile land. Settlement goes back to the Bronze Age, and Melite probably became Malta’s main administrative centre in the Phoenician period.",
        "Archaeology suggests that the area outside Melite’s walls was first used as surface quarries. The stone removed may have been used to build ancient Melite. The quarry terraces were later reused as a burial ground during the later Phoenician and Punic periods, around the fourth and third centuries BC.",
        "The cemetery developed from smaller shaft-and-chamber tombs into larger communal catacombs. Earlier Romans often preferred visible family tombs, but later Roman and Byzantine communities increasingly preferred group burial: a community of the dead.",
        "The cemetery was used by pagans, Christians and Jews, and it is one of Malta’s main physical sources for the Byzantine period. By the end of Byzantine rule, its use as a burial ground had ended. Later medieval reuse transformed some areas into churches or oratories.",
        "The recent archaeological project confirmed quarrying, shaft-and-chamber tombs, cremation deposits, infant amphora burials, dumps, repeated tomb reuse and surviving in-situ remains. Tomb 15 produced a female skeleton with vessels, a net-weaving needle, a face pendant, a glass bottle and a bone hairpin still beneath the skull. Tomb 31 produced coffin clamps, bronze needles, glass bottles, lamps, a coin, imported ceramics, cremated remains and scientifically excavated wood fragments.",
        "One rare personal detail is the lead inscription mentioning Fufi/Fufia Galena and Custus/Custodius Adienus, husband and wife, who acted in memory of Valeria, described as well-deserving. Valeria is therefore one of the few named people known from the Rabat cemeteries.",
        "Conservation starts on site as soon as fragile objects are discovered. Artefacts are lifted carefully, taken to laboratories, cleaned, conserved and sometimes excavated further in controlled conditions. This applies not only to display objects but to any material that can preserve information."
