        "Additional NotebookLM notes to add",
        "• Preparation & Lying-in-State: For elite funerals, the body could lie in state for up to eight days, placed on a couch or table in the atrium, the central heart of the home. This allowed political peers and visitors, possibly even from Sicily, to pay their respects.",
        "• The Pompa / Procession: Funerals traditionally took place at night by torchlight. This may have had practical reasons, such as hygiene and smell, and religious reasons, such as avoiding the evil eye.",
        "• Musicians: The procession could be led with flutes and horns.",
        "• Mourners (Bikkejja): Paid professional mourners followed the procession crying. Their tears were sometimes collected in jars and buried with the deceased to show how loved the person was.",
        "• Actors / Mimes: Actors could mimic the deceased’s mannerisms, keeping their memory alive during the walk to the tomb.",
        "• The Family: The funeral involved ritual subversion: women covered their heads with veils while men left theirs uncovered, the opposite of daily custom.",
        "• The Mask Bearer: The first-born son or next in line could walk carrying wax masks of previous generations, creating a physical link with the ancestors.",
        "• Freed Slaves: Freed slaves could serve as pallbearers. Their uncovered heads and thoughtful posture symbolised their new freedom, but also the uncertainty of a future without a master.",
        "Additional YouTube notes to add",
        "Death was treated as a major ritual moment. Mortuary rites helped mourners express grief, accept death, celebrate the deceased and reinforce beliefs about the afterlife.",
        "Across cultures, three elements are usually present: funeral ceremonies, a sacred or set-apart place for the dead, and remembrance of the dead.",
        "The body was washed, sometimes with perfumed oils, and wrapped in cloth bandages. Lamentation could include sackcloth, tearing hair and beating breasts.",
        "Objects could accompany the dead because they were believed useful or protective in the afterlife: food, coins, ceramic vessels, figurines and personal possessions. Magical or protective objects could also be included.",
        "Egyptian amulets were common protective talismans. Phoenician and Punic culture absorbed Egyptian religious elements, including protective gods and goddesses. A small papyrus found in a tomb outside Rabat includes a prayer invoking Isis to defeat an enemy blocking a sea journey to the underworld.",
        "Roman funeral ritual began at the deathbed. Relatives gathered, the closest relative gave the farewell kiss and closed the eyes, and mourners began the conclamatio, repeatedly calling the dead person’s name to announce death within the household.",
        "A wax death mask could be taken and kept in the atrium with ancestral masks, linking the deceased to the family line and cult of ancestors.",
        "A coin could be placed with the deceased to pay Charon, the ferryman who carried the dead across the River Styx to Hades. This was similar to earlier Phoenician customs.",
        "Poorer people could be taken for burial at night in simple processions. Wealthier citizens could have a public and theatrical funeral with musicians, paid mourners, mimes, freed slaves and relatives.",
        "A professional funerary organiser, transcribed as libitinarius, could be hired to arrange the preparations. At the cemetery, the body could be placed in a grave, built tomb or sarcophagus, although sarcophagi have not been found in the Maltese Islands."
