        "Additional NotebookLM notes to add",
        "• Modern Infrastructure: The garden contains ventilation holes that once allowed air into the subterranean galleries. The Visitor Centre pavilions present some of the most striking modern discoveries.",
        "• The Story of “Elsa”: In 2013, while preparing the Visitor Centre foundations, archaeologists used Ground Penetrating Radar (GPR) and located a 1st- to 2nd-century CE shaft-and-chamber tomb.",
        "• Forensic Reconstruction: Inside was a skeleton nicknamed “Elsa”. Beside her were a bone hairpin, a face pendant and a glass unguentarium. Her face was reconstructed using clay layers over a 3D model of her skull, with an anthropometric study of contemporary Sicilian women used as reference. The result gives visitors a direct human connection to the past.",
        "• The Social Aspect: The pavilions show how catacombs inspired literature and traditions about the underworld, martyrs and the 17th-century “martyrdom mania”, when bones were uncovered and sold as holy relics.",
        "Additional YouTube notes to add",
        "The garden and pavilions help present the site as a long-lived landscape rather than just tunnels: quarry, cemetery, catacomb system, reused space and modern conserved heritage site.",
        "The Social Pavilion is useful for explaining memory and folklore. Dark underground places fascinated and frightened people, and the catacombs inspired local stories.",
        "Popular stories claimed that Christians hid there from Roman or Muslim persecution, that giants dug the caves, that a pig entered the catacombs and emerged elsewhere, that a class of children was lost and their voices could still be heard, and that St Agatha used the catacombs to hide from persecution.",
        "The transcript stresses that many of these stories are fiction. The catacombs were primarily cemeteries, not hiding places. The Romans knew where they were, and hiding among decomposing bodies would not have been practical.",
        "Even fictional stories matter because they shaped local memory and sometimes entered publications as if they were fact.",
