    "6": {
      "title": "Burials",
      "paragraphs": [
        "– talk about burial rites and the different types of tombs found in Malta [TVPS the Arcosolium – floor tombs - Window Tombs etc.]",
        "Acrosolium in front of the agape - shows feasting was a great deal",
        "Tables indicate some kind of offering or feasting.",
        "We have no evidence but, comparing to example Pompei, however, they are still similar.",
        "(ma tantx fimtha din il bicca l ahwa)",
        "Fossores - were grave diggers",
        "Used pick and axe",
        "By time, gained status, like a construction company. The elite would hire them to build more tombs for them.",
        "Collegiata of elite families would contact the fossores.",
        "They had a map of the tombs; allowed foreigners travelling here to pay their respects",
        "Wall & floor tombs - cheapest burials: lower part of the elite",
        "Imagine the smell, insects and cockroaches found here. During this period of time, people did not live long - till around 40.",
        "Society was plagued by viruses, many through use of such catacombs - feasting with fermenting bodies of the dead.",
        "People did not have our knowledge of cleanliness.",
        "Covered with slavs or terracatorra, then sealed with plaster.",
        "Holes in the wall for light, lanterns, candles.",
        "Important: in early Christianity, at one point there was an order to stop using agape tables and so most of them were destroyed. This was because feasting became exaggerated, including orgies. Malta did not have orgies so they did not destroy them.",
        "Additional NotebookLM notes to add",
        "• Preparation Rituals: At death, the nearest relative would close the eyes and mouth, sometimes attempting a final kiss to “catch the soul”. Women of the family washed the corpse, preferably with seawater, as a rite of passage.",
        "• Dressing the Body: Men could be buried in a Roman toga, while those who died young were sometimes laid out in wedding attire.",
        "• Window Tombs: A common tomb type with a square opening, often 80-100cm above floor level, leading to an oval chamber with rock-cut pillows.",
        "• Arcosolium: A tomb with an arch and sill cut into the wall. Maltese versions are smaller than Sicilian examples and usually held only two or three people.",
        "• Floor Tombs / Forma: Simple graves cut into the floor, later sometimes reused as ossuaries to hold bones from several individuals.",
        "• Rock Pillows: Semicircular cavities in the stone where the head was rested. These indicate the capacity of each grave.",
        "Additional YouTube notes to add",
        "Choosing a tomb meant choosing the deceased’s eternal resting place. Location, tomb type, decoration and closure all reflected status, wealth and sometimes family identity.",
        "Roman law forbade burial within city limits, so cemeteries and catacombs were outside the city walls. Wealthier families could choose private catacombs or prominent positions within public ones.",
        "The cheapest Maltese catacomb tomb type was the floor tomb, a rectangular pit cut into the ground. These were often dug into corridors, meaning visitors physically walked over them.",
        "Loculi were also relatively cheap. In Malta they were mainly for infants and children, unlike Rome and Sicily where they were more broadly used. Their number reflects high infant mortality and limited space.",
        "Arcosolia were more expensive and very common. An arcosolium is a tomb cut under an arch, with the body placed in troughs or on a shelf. Rooms with arcosolia on three sides could be sealed off from the rest of the catacomb.",
        "Window tombs were more sophisticated and seem to be a local development. They consist of a burial chamber accessed through a window-like opening inside a small niche. They are common in rural Maltese catacombs but less frequent in the main Rabat catacombs.",
        "Window tombs could look intimate and elegant, often with small flanking pillars and carved or painted decoration.",
        "Burial rites usually included washing the body, treatment with oils where possible, wrapping in cloth, lamentation, and the placing of objects such as food, coins, ceramics, figurines, lamps, unguentaria, personal belongings and protective amulets.",
        "Christian symbols include the cross, palm branch, fish and anchor. However, because pagans and Christians used similar tomb architecture and funerary meals, symbols are needed before identifying a tomb as Christian."
      ]
    },
